 "rulebook": "FIVB Official Volleyball Rules 2025-2028: Rule 1 (the court 18 m x 9 m, the service zone 9 m wide behind the end line), Rule 2 (the men's net, top at 2.43 m, the antennas), Rule 3 (the ball: 65-67 cm round, 260-280 g), Rule 8 (ball in and out: the lines belong to the court; a ball that touches an antenna or crosses the net's plane outside the crossing space is out), Rule 10.2 (the ball may touch the net while crossing it), Rule 12.4 (the service: the ball is hit with one hand or any part of the arm after being tossed or released from the hands; one toss only; the server does not touch the court or the floor outside the service zone at the hit; the hit comes within 8 s of the whistle)",
 "deviations": [
  "Rule 12.4.1 (tossed or released from the hand(s)): the robot's hands are flat palm pads without fingers and without grip; the ball starts resting on the open left palm, turned up, and only its weight and friction keep it there (it rolls off a palm that tilts by more than about 2 degrees or moves sideways). The toss is the arm's own movement throwing the ball up off the palm (or letting it fall from it). The ball has left the hand when it has touched no part of the robot for 0.03 s; the release is the end of its last contact; until then only the hands may touch it. The palm absorbs the ball as a hand does (the palm-ball contact of dunk-realgrasp: a ball thrown at a still palm keeps 0.12 of its speed, in the world's elliptic friction cones); every other contact keeps the ball's rule-book rebound.",
  "Rule 12.4.1 (tossed, then hit): a referee's eye for 'tossed, then hit' is replaced by numbers: after the release the ball must be clear of the robot for at least 0.1 s before the hit, it may move at most 5.0 m/s just before the hit (the speed of a ball that has fallen about 1.3 m: a toss, not a throw), the hit's contacts last at most 0.1 s, and the ball must leave the hit faster than it arrived.",
  "Rule 12.4.3 (the server's feet) is judged at the first contact of the hit, or at the take-off before it when the robot is in the air; the whistle is the start of the episode.",
  "No opponents (nobody receives the serve), no wind."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Serve the volleyball: from behind the end line, toss it up off your open left palm and hit it with one hand or arm over the net into the opponent's court.",
  "rules": "You stand behind the end line of an official volleyball court (9.0 m by 9.0 m on each side of the net), facing the net, midway between the side lines; the end line's outer edge is 0.5 m ahead of your pelvis and the net is 9.0 m beyond it, its top 2.43 m high, with an antenna above each side line. A volleyball (0.21 m across, 0.27 kg, in air) starts resting on your open left palm, turned up. Your hands have no fingers and no grip (the grip columns of the action rows do nothing in this task): only the ball's weight and friction keep it on the palm, and it rolls off a palm that tilts or moves sideways. Until the toss only your hands (the palm pads and the wrists) may touch the ball, and the ball may not touch the floor. In order: (1) the toss: the ball has touched no part of you for 0.03 s; the release is the end of its last contact with a hand. For 0.1 s from the release the hand that held it may still touch it (it follows the toss up; any other touch in that time is already the hit), and the ball must be clear of that hand when that time ends. (2) the hit: the next contact of the ball, with one hand or one arm of yours (hand, forearm or upper arm of the same side), within 8.0 s of the start. The ball must have been clear of you for at least 0.1 s before the hit (counted from the end of the last touch of the toss), moving at most 5.0 m/s just before it; the hit's contacts last at most 0.1 s (it is over when the ball has been clear of you for 0.03 s), and the ball leaves the hit faster than it arrived. At the first contact of the hit no foot of yours touches the court (the end line included) or the floor beyond the extensions of the side lines; if you are in the air, this holds for where you took off. (3) the ball crosses the net's plane above the net and between the antennas; it may touch the net. (4) the ball's first contact with the floor is in the opponent's court (9.0 m by 9.0 m, lines included): that is the success. You fail if the hit does not come within 8.0 s, if after the toss the ball touches the floor or any other part of you before the hit, if it is hit before it has been clear for 0.1 s, if it moves faster than 5.0 m/s just before the hit, if the hit is made with more than one arm or another part, lasts longer than 0.1 s or does not add speed, if you touch the ball again or hold it again, if a part of you other than the hands touches it before the toss, at a foot fault, if the ball crosses the net's plane under the net or outside the antennas or touches an antenna, if it lands anywhere else, if you fall (your pelvis lower than 0.35 m above the floor, or any part of you other than the feet on the floor), or after 12 s.",
  "venue": "You face the net (+x), which is 9.5 m ahead of you, from the middle of the service zone. Your upper arms hang and your forearms point forward; the left forearm is turned palm up (its elbow roll at -90 degrees) and the ball rests on the middle of the left palm, its centre about 1.3 m above the floor, 0.43 m ahead of your pelvis and 0.21 m to its left. The start command keeps the palm level under the ball (the joint table's start angles are where the arm stands with the ball on it; the command differs from them by the arm's sag).",
  "difficulty": "The ball has to leave the hit at about 11 m/s (10.8 m/s from 2.2 m up, 11.5 m/s underarm from 1 m) to clear a 2.43 m net 9 m away and still come down inside the far court: a fist at 6 to 7 m/s, about the most a full swing of this arm gives, through a ball the robot has tossed itself off a flat palm (straight up: a palm that moves sideways leaves the ball behind), at the right instant, on a 67 kg robot whose stand has no balance feedback."
 },
